{:edb/db-before-t 1836 :edb/db-after-t 1837 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535149 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:03:58.623Z" 13194139535149 true] [17592186228094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333916 13194139535149 true] [17592186228094 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333916 13194139535149 true] [17592186228860 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2 - xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139535149 true] [17592186228860 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2 - xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2)$. Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139535149 false] [17592186228860 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((2 - xy^{2}) + (2 - x_{new}(y_{new})^{2})) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4 - xy^{2} - x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2 - 0.5xy^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 2 - 0.5xy^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2 = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.5 \\cdot 2(y_{new})^{2} + y_{new} - 1 - 2 + 0.5 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1^{2} \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{2} + y_{new} - 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nWe'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 2(y_\\text{new}) + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 2.5} {2(y_\\text{new})_n + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1) = 1$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y_{new})_{1} &= 1 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 1.166667 \\\\ (y_{new})_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 1.158333 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{4} \\mid \\approx 1.158312\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1.16}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535149 false] [17592186228860 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2-x{y}^{2})+(2-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-x{y}^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) \\\\  & =2-0.5x{y}^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2-0.5x{y}^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5\\cdot 2\\,({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-2+0.5\\cdot 1\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 2(y_\\text{new}) + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1}\n= (y_\\text{new})_n\n- \\dfrac{((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 2.5}\n       {2(y_\\text{new})_n + 1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1) = 1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.166\\,667 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.158\\,333 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.158\\,312\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1.16},$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535149 true] [17592186228861 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139535149 true] [17592186228861 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1$, the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n1. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n2. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n3. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n4. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139535149 false] [17592186228861 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2y^{2} - \\sqrt{x} + 2(y_{new})^{2} - \\sqrt{x_{new}}) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= y^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} + (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + y^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} + (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{2} - 1 - 1^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535149 false] [17592186228861 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\sqrt{x}+2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}} = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1-{1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535149 true] [17592186229108 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333923 13194139535149 true] [17592186229108 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333930 13194139535149 true] [17592186229108 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333937 13194139535149 true] [17592186229108 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333944 13194139535149 true] [17592186229109 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333951 13194139535149 true] [17592186229109 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333954 13194139535149 true] [17592186229109 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333958 13194139535149 true] [17592186229109 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333962 13194139535149 true] [17592186229609 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535149 true] [17592186229609 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - y^{3}, y(0.5) = 1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1)$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535149 false] [17592186229609 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0.5)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535149 true] [17592186229609 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-{y}^{3})+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5x-0.25{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5x-0.25{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25(1)^{3}-1-0.5\\cdot 1-0.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe