592186333943 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333943 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333943 1111 #uuid "6a616447-b9b8-5764-bfe0-847e9151ffd4" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333944 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333945 13194139535149 true] [17592186333944 1089 "q-334075" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333944 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535149 true] [17592186333944 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333944 1113 #uuid "8c3a7627-c377-5c3a-851c-bcadd27ed02f" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333944 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2(2)^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1-2(1)^{2}(1)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333946 13194139535149 true] [17592186333945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333947 13194139535149 true] [17592186333945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333948 13194139535149 true] [17592186333945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333949 13194139535149 true] [17592186333945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333950 13194139535149 true] [17592186333945 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535149 true] [17592186333945 1041 "selection" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333945 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333948 13194139535149 true] [17592186333945 1110 #uuid "c422d4a1-b9e2-50e8-8992-162a9a762d4b" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333946 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333946 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333946 1111 #uuid "0ea3a259-9de2-5c30-acf0-969554ff4fcd" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333947 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333947 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333947 1111 #uuid "ac071946-c43b-5f00-a590-1c5af5682f9e" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333948 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333948 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333948 1111 #uuid "babc3af7-b8e9-54c1-8dce-0c7280c00c60" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333949 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333949 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333949 1111 #uuid "e31e9de5-e78f-5477-87f1-ce14ebb8ddf8" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333950 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333950 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333950 1111 #uuid "743a50e9-4c15-5856-88d4-fa27e7a977a3" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333951 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333952 13194139535149 true] [17592186333951 1089 "q-334197" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333951 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535149 true] [17592186333951 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+4{y}^{2},\\,y(0.5)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333951 1113 #uuid "60f08bf3-3155-5b9a-98b6-e147ed21f307" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333951 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ starting from $x=0.5,$ we use a step size\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-x+4{y}^{2})+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in this step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+0+0-0.25(0.5)-0.25(1) \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y(0.5)=0$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})}{{g}^{′}(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}^{\\,2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.375}{2({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1}=y(0.5)=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈-0.375 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-0.29464 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-0.29058 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{5} & ≈-0.29057\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈-0.29,$\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535149 true] [17592186333952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333953 13194139535149 true] [17592186333952 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535149 true] [17592186333952 1041 "field-1" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333952 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333953 13194139535149 true] [17592186333952 1110 #uuid "b7422642-e58a-58ca-badf-cda7c998faa3" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333953 1034 "-0.29" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333953 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333953 1111 #uuid "52386a81-83af-5451-910b-16dae66bbc34" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333954 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333955 13194139535149 true] [17592186333954 1089 "q-334426" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333954 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535149 true] [17592186333954 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.4).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333954 1113 #uuid "0037370c-41e1-56de-bcdc-f8c10918ac15" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333954 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.4,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.4-0=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+{y}^{2})+({x}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $5$ to eliminate the decimals, we obtain\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5y+x+{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.4$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5y+x+{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.4=0.4.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5\\cdot 0+0+{0}^{2}+0.4 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=0$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=2({y}_{\\text{new}})-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-5({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+0.4}{2({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-5}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=0,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =0.08 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.081\\,322 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.081\\,323\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.4)≈0.08,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535149 true] [17592186333955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333956 13194139535149 true] [17592186333955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333957 13194139535149 true] [17592186333955 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535149 true] [17592186333955 1041 "field-1" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333955 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333956 13194139535149 true] [17592186333955 1110 #uuid "7c72a69c-15b6-5f76-a3d5-43f62e6c6f54" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333956 1034 "0.08" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333956 1111 #uuid "68284d95-ee18-5ff7-a2e6-ff8199f9c578" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333957 1034 "0.09" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333957 1111 #uuid "4f033d62-cc65-57a8-98e7-48568bedd9db" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333958 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333959 13194139535149 true] [17592186333958 1089 "q-334469" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333958 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535149 true] [17592186333958 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333958 1113 #uuid "51bc4d54-4dd4-521c-9e72-90bab859c94d" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333958 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x-{y}^{3})+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-1.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-1.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5(1)^{3}-1-1.5\\cdot 1-1.5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5-1-1.5 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.179\\,747 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.179\\,509\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.18,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535149 true] [17592186333959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333960 13194139535149 true] [17592186333959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333961 13194139535149 true] [17592186333959 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535149 true] [17592186333959 1041 "field-1" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333959 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333960 13194139535149 true] [17592186333959 1110 #uuid "60086b88-7e2f-50c6-95fa-939ab8812401" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333960 1034 "1.18" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333960 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333960 1111 #uuid "2985367d-cb43-5c51-8eed-abbb0db812d5" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333961 1034 "1.20" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333961 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535149 true] [17592186333961 1111 #uuid "efe9b5ca-8337-5cba-8f91-55c4ce871ce5" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333962 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333963 13194139535149 true] [17592186333962 1089 "q-334473" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333962 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535149 true] [17592186333962 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+{y}^{3},\\,y(1)=-1.5\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333962 1113 #uuid "d6c43cc2-f4b5-59ac-b529-c1877515e05f" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333962 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x+{y}^{3})+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+(-1.5)+1+0.5(-1.5)^{3}+2 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.5+1-1.6875+2 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.1875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-1.5$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.1875}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1)=-1.5,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =-1.5 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈-1.342\\,105 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-1.310\\,301 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-1.309\\,019\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-1.31,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535149 true] [17592186333963 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333964 13194139535149 true] [17592186333963 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535149 true] [17592186333963 1041 "field-1" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333963 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333964 13194139535149 true] [17592186333963 1110 #uuid "ae3c4ab2-0bd3-5aed-b6fd-0ca5533de55b" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333964 1034 "-1.31" 13194139535149 true] [17592186333964 1103 #edb/ref 1759